  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe local area will be in the warm sector ahead of an\napproaching cold front tonight through Tuesday before the front\nfinally pushes southeast across the area late Tuesday into Wednesday.\nA shortwave will cross Lake Erie and NE OH/NW PA tonight into \nearly Tuesday morning, resulting in a chance of isolated showers\nand possibly a few rumbles of thunder across the northeastern \nportion of the local area.\n\nDeep warm air advection is expected on Tuesday with widespread\ntemperatures in the 80s expected. Several locations across\nnorthern Ohio will likely reach the mid to upper 80s (best\nchance along/west of I-71), which will challenge daily high\ntemperature records (see Climate section at the bottom of this\nAFD). In addition to the warm temperatures, dew points will\nclimb well into the 60s Tuesday afternoon so it will certainly\nfeel muggier than the recent stretch of crisp early fall\nweather. It will also be quite breezy with southwest wind gusts\nto 20 to 30 mph expected during peak diurnal mixing late Tuesday\nmorning into Tuesday afternoon.\n\nThe best chance of precipitation will arrive with the cold front\nlate Tuesday night into Wednesday. There will be instability and\nplenty of moisture across the area so thunderstorms are \npossible, although widespread cloud cover and showers early in \nthe day will mitigate the risk of stronger thunderstorms. Rain \nmay be moderate to heavy at times Wednesday morning into early \nWednesday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA brief break in precip is expected Wednesday evening through\nThursday morning, but the next (and more potent) system will\nmove into the region starting Thursday afternoon. Showers are\nexpected and a few thunderstorms are possible as the system's\ncold front approaches and moves east across the area Thursday \nafternoon through early Friday with scattered lake-enhanced\nshowers continuing through Friday and maybe as late as Saturday\nas the associated strong upper trough lags behind the initial \nsurface front. It will be quite windy as strong high pressure \nattempts to build east into the region Friday and gusts to 35 \nto 40 mph are likely areawide Friday afternoon with locally \nhigher gusts possible close to Lake Erie. The back side of the \nsystem will usher some of the coldest temperatures of the season\nthus far into the area with widespread highs in the upper 40s \ninto the 50s expected Saturday. Frost headlines are possible as \ntemperatures dip into the upper 30s Friday night, although \nthere is a higher chance of frost and even freeze headlines as \ntemps drop into the upper 20s to mid 30s Saturday night. \nTemperatures might even be cold enough across portions of NW PA \nto warrant a low-end chance of the first few snowflakes of the \nseason mixing in with rain early Saturday. Temperatures will \nbegin to recover Sunday as the high takes control of the eastern\nCONUS.",
